First create the table with table, tr, th, and td tag-pairs. Use the first row for headings and the remaining rows for data. Then put a style tag before the table and apply a border to the table, header cells, and data cells. A background colour can also be assigned to the cells. For example:

<style>
  table, th, td {
    border: 1px solid black;
  }
  th, td {
    background-color: aqua;
  }
</style>
<table>
  <tr><th>Student Name</th><th>Class</th><th>Fee Dues</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Alia</td><td>9</td><td>200</td></tr>
</table>

The table tag-pair allocates the table, tr defines rows, th defines the header cells, and td defines ordinary data cells.

Keep the current colour and index in previousColor and previousIndex before the existing button changes them. Add a Revert button and define a function that restores those saved values and updates the visible content. For example:

<script>
  let previousColor = document.body.style.backgroundColor;
  let previousIndex = currentIndex;

  function revert() {
    document.body.style.backgroundColor = previousColor;
    currentIndex = previousIndex;
    document.getElementById("dynamicContent").textContent = currentIndex;
  }
</script>
<button onclick="revert()">Revert</button>

Before each forward change, assign the current colour and index to the previous values so Revert restores the immediately preceding state. The chapter's dynamic-page example uses document.body.style.backgroundColor to change the page colour and a button calling descOrder() to change the displayed index order, so the new button can be kept separate and call revert().
